Lab: Preventing a Switching Loop

Objective

Establish connectivity between all network devices while preventing a switching loop caused by redundant switch connections.

Environment

Tools Used

Router
Switches x3

Operating System

N/A

Skills Practiced

Switch Port Configuration
Layer 2 Switching
Network Topology
Switching Loop Prevention
Physical Link Verification

Actions Performed

1. Started in a network closet with one router and 3 switches. Each device has patch cables already configured, but none of the ports are activated
2. First, starting with the router, I activated both the internet port and the LAN port. The LAN port is connected to Switch 1, port 23.
3. On Switch 1, I activated Port 23 to establish connectivity with the router. I then activated Ports 1, 3, and 5 for the connected computers. Of the two switch uplinks, I activated the right uplink connecting Switch 1 to Switch 2. 
4. On Switch 2, I activated Ports 1, 3, and 5 for the connected computers. I activated the left uplink connecting Switch 2 to Switch 3. 
5. On Switch 3, I activated Ports 1, 3, and 5 for the connected computers. I activated the left uplink connecting Switch 3 to Switch 2. I left the right uplink to Switch 1 disabled to prevent the redundant connection from creating a switching loop.
6. Documented findings and ended the lab

Commands Used

N/A

Verification

Verified the required ports were activated by confirming the green status indicator on each port. Confirmed the redundant uplink between Switch 1 and Switch 3 remained disabled to prevent a switching loop. 

Issues Encountered

None

Resolution

Successfully activated the required router, switch, and endpoint ports while preventing a switching loop by leaving the redundant Switch 1-to-Switch 3 uplink disabled.

Key Takeaways

- Redundant switch connections can improve network resilience but may create Layer 2 switching loops if not properly managed.
- Switch uplinks must be planned carefully when multiple switches are interconnected.
- A redundant physical connection should not simply be activated without considering its effect on the network topology.
- Port status indicators provide a quick method for verifying physical link status.
- Understanding the network topology before enabling switch ports can help prevent connectivity problems.


